WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.960
Conditionals, like if statements, are great for getting

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:12.904
something to happen when a condition is true.

00:00:12.904 --> 00:00:14.744
But what if the condition is false

00:00:14.744 --> 00:00:16.964
and we want something else to happen?

00:00:16.964 --> 00:00:19.824
That's when the if/else statement comes in handy.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:24.968
At first, an if/else statement looks a lot like a regular if statement,

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:29.953
but by clicking the plus button down here, we can add another condition, the else.

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:35.433
The if/else statement will do one thing if the Boolean expression is true, and will

00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:38.433
do something else if the Boolean is false.

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:44.937
In this case, we're going to create a sprite in the middle of the screen.

00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:49.922
Next, we'll add a draw function, followed by a white

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:55.682
background. Now we'll add an if/else statement by dragging out the if block

00:00:55.682 --> 00:00:59.907
and clicking the little plus button to give us an else.

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:04.987
Let's give the conditional something to check, like whether the right arrow button is being pressed.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:14.891
If the right arrow is being pressed, then the sprite will go right.

00:01:14.891 --> 00:01:19.876
But if the right button isn't being pressed,

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:24.036
then the Boolean will be false, and the code in the else section will run.

00:01:25.036 --> 00:01:27.676
In this case, it will move the sprite to the left.

00:01:29.036 --> 00:01:29.860
Finally, we'll draw

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.620
the sprite and see what develops.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:47.845
Now that you can use the else statement, your programs can perform twice as many actions and reactions as before.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:52.409
(upbeat music)
